ui-skills

Nothing here is a copy of another skill's files. What follows is what was read
on 2026-10-27, what was taken as an idea, and how it was changed. Every source
below was read at the source; docs/source-research.md records the reading.

Taken as ideas, reworded and reduced:

- EnchStyle/ui-ux-audit-skill, MIT.
  https://github.com/EnchStyle/ui-ux-audit-skill
  Taken: the four severity bands judged by harm rather than by effort, the
  practice of listing what not to flag, and measuring the render before
  judging it. Changed: the bands were rewritten, the scoring out of 100 was
  dropped, and the categories were replaced by the six kinds of finding used
  here. Its uicheck.py was not copied: scripts/measure.js was written for this
  skill and runs in the browser through the Playwright MCP server instead of
  needing Python Playwright.

- andrew-shwetzer/human-experience, MIT.
  https://github.com/andrew-shwetzer/human-experience
  Taken: walking a journey step by step as the customer, with what they expect
  set against what happens, and the rule that a reported feeling must name
  what caused it. Changed: the scorecard out of 35, the HTML report, the
  iteration loop and the AgentMail signup testing were all left out, and its
  claim to replace user testing is contradicted here rather than repeated.

- coreyhaines31/marketingskills, skills/customer-research, MIT.
  https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills
  Taken: confidence bands for a research claim, the sample-bias warnings, and
  treating fetched reviews and posts as untrusted data. Changed: reduced to
  the fact, assumption and invention distinction in
  references/product-thinking.md, which points at the skill itself for the
  research method.

- Leonxlnx/taste-skill, MIT. https://github.com/Leonxlnx/taste-skill
  Taken: that a page's idea comes from the subject rather than from its
  category, and that stripping ornament without adding substance makes a page
  worse. Changed: written as two paragraphs in
  references/directions.md, which refers to the skill for the method.
  Its direction library and scripts are not reproduced.

- pbakaus/impeccable, Apache-2.0. https://github.com/pbakaus/impeccable
  Taken: that the right ambition depends on what the visitor is there to do —
  to be persuaded, or to get work done. Changed: used as the split between
  the landing-page and dashboard guidance in
  references/product-thinking.md. None of its prose, its command set or its
  tooling is reused.

- nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill, MIT.
  https://github.com/nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill
  Taken: part of the numeric defaults in references/directions.md, by way of
  the author's own blueprint skill, which adapted them first. Changed:
  reduced to one table, reworded, and merged with the WCAG and Apple figures
  that are nobody's skill.

- jakubkrehel/make-interfaces-feel-better, MIT.
  https://github.com/jakubkrehel/make-interfaces-feel-better
  Taken: the craft values in references/craft.md — the three-layer
  shadow-as-border in oklch, concentric radii, scale(0.96) on press, the
  staggered enter with blur, tabular figures, optical alignment, icon stroke
  matched to text weight, and hit areas extended past the visible control.
  Changed: reduced to one page, rewritten, and the review format and the
  per-category reference files are not reproduced.

- jakubkrehel/skills, MIT. https://github.com/jakubkrehel/skills
  Taken into ui-review, section 5. A cue on each scenario axis, and the rule
  that a dropped axis is named. The content-shape axis: emoji, right-to-left,
  mixed direction, tall scripts. The container tested as a fixed box rather
  than a viewport resize, the flex-sibling squeeze included. Ten times the
  realistic item count. The warning that re-declaring dark tokens under a
  class observes a different component.
  Taken into ui-review/references/changes.md. Resolve a base, and never fall
  back to HEAD~1..HEAD. Expand a changed file to the surfaces it renders in.
  Read the removed side of each hunk against a signal table. Clear a signal
  that an equivalent replacement explains. Status every finding by cause. Keep
  the review read-only, the checkout included.
  Changed: rewritten, and reduced to two places in one skill. Not reproduced:
  the per-domain better-* skills, the severity scale, the coverage table, the
  verdict, and the teardown, state-machine, break, variant and
  explain-interface skills.

- emilkowalski/skills, MIT. https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills
  Taken: ease-out over ease-in and why, the duration bands per interaction,
  never animating from scale(0), and transitions for interruptible state
  against keyframes for sequences. Changed: reduced to the motion section of
  references/craft.md in our own words; none of its animation recipes,
  component guidance or prose is reused.

- benjitaylor/skills, the stageshow skill, MIT.
  https://github.com/benjitaylor/skills/tree/main/stageshow
  Read in full on 2026-11-12, at the repository.
  Taken into references/craft.md: the spacing and alignment numbers and the
  faults they name — the 12px word inset and 8px floor, 8 to 12px between a
  control and the edge above it, 4 to 6px of ink between a title and its
  subtitle, 12px row padding and the three-times-its-text row, a status mark
  6px clear and inside the row's inset, one grid template down a list, no two
  edges 1 to 3px apart, a unit kept with its number, a body aligned to the
  name rather than the avatar, centring a label on its cap height and an
  amount on its digits, fitting content rather than widening a container,
  flex: none on a fixed box, and a popover that holds still once open. Also
  the emphasis rules: no accent ring standing in for hierarchy, no redundant
  tonal border, a filled button keeping its own colour under the pointer, and
  nothing translucent overlapping. Also four icon rules: one size per surface,
  filled at 14px and under, no filled glyph beside an outline one, and one
  opaque colour per icon.
  Taken into scripts/measure.js: that these are the parts of craft that are
  numbers, and five of them worth measuring — clipped, tight inset, uneven
  padding, near-tangent and a wrapped unit. The detectors were written for
  this skill against our own fixtures; none of its code is reused, and its
  film scripts need playwright-core and a React kit that this skill does not
  have.
  Taken into references/playwright.md: that motion is filmed rather than
  inferred, that every appear and disappear is sampled closely, that a loop is
  captured with the start of the next one, and that a clean capture is not an
  approval. The mechanics here are getAnimations and a requestAnimationFrame
  sampler through the Playwright MCP server, which was measured on 2026-11-12,
  not its film.mjs.
  Not taken: the whole purpose of the skill. Stageshow builds faux product UI
  as illustration, which gates.md rule 37 forbids in a real product, and its
  kit, examples, playground, teaching pairs and framing rules all serve that
  purpose. Its faux-specific rules are not here: no masthead for a fictional
  name, word budgets, placeholder bars, drawn device chrome, or a faux app's
  title as a div rather than a heading.

- kosta4a/tactile-design, MIT, "Copyright (c) 2026 the tactile-design
  authors". https://github.com/kosta4a/tactile-design
  Read in full on 2026-11-13, at the repository: its LICENSE, README, SKILL.md
  and the seven reference files (about 180KB). Its landing page at
  www.4hands.dev/tactile-design states no licence and carries no values; the
  LICENSE in the repository is the licence that was read.
  Taken into references/tactile.md, as a named direction rather than a rule:
  its house style with its values, which it calls tactile, layered and quiet.
  The twelve-step neutral and its jobs, the surface roles, the step-shift
  rule, the fill-edge-drop recipe drawn from the parent surface, the crisp
  shadow ladder, the radius ladder from one control radius, the type scale and
  the one-weight rule, the control recipes and sizes, the motion tokens and
  durations, the phone sizes and the sheet, and its never-ship list. Rewritten
  in this skill's own words and shortened to about a fifth of the source; the
  hex values of Radix sand are not reproduced, and the file says to measure
  steps 9 and 10 rather than trusting them.
  Taken into references/craft.md, as style-independent craft: the worst-case
  data rules (32 characters then truncate, compact notation past six digits,
  an en dash for a missing value, Intl.PluralRules, min-width: 0), the touch
  rules (hover under a fine pointer only, the press carrying the hover look,
  no tap highlight, touch-action, overscroll-behavior, 100dvh, safe-area
  insets, 16px fields), the sheet drag numbers (4px slop, velocity over the
  last 100ms, close past min(64px, 15%) or a 0.11 px/ms flick, the upward
  rubber band and the two pointer-capture traps), the travel and blur ladders,
  the press-scale ladder by target size, and reduced motion as softening
  rather than deletion. Its curve for user-caused motion,
  cubic-bezier(.23, 1, .32, 1), is the one already in this file from another
  source, which is evidence for it rather than a new rule.
  Taken into references/gates.md: three copy rules, numbered 48 to 50 —
  sentence case with a real ellipsis and no em dash, a button label that is a
  verb plus its object, and a confirmation that states its consequence. Also a
  clause in Floor rule 3: a label shown before the real state is known is a
  false result.
  Where it disagrees: it draws every light shadow in the neutral's darkest
  ink, where references/craft.md calls for pure black at low alpha. Both
  readings are recorded in craft.md rather than one being hidden. It also
  overrides four rows of the numbers table in references/directions.md and two
  Reflex rules in references/gates.md; tactile.md lists each override, and a
  waiver is only legitimate once DESIGN.md records it.
  Not taken: the 160KB of per-component detail, which stays in the source and
  is pointed at from tactile.md rather than copied; the Radix sand hex values;
  its Tailwind v4 mapping; and its claim on a default typeface, because
  gates.md rule 29 holds that a display face is a decision.
  Its own credits, carried forward: motion principles and the sheet drag
  conventions follow Emil Kowalski (animations.dev), the twelve-step scale
  structure is Radix Colors, and the touch-target and sheet conventions are
  Apple's Human Interface Guidelines.

- google-labs-code/design.md, Apache-2.0.
  https://github.com/google-labs-code/design.md
  Taken: the DESIGN.md token schema used in references/design-system.md — the
  front-matter YAML layer, the colors, typography, spacing, rounded and
  components groups, the {path.to.token} reference form, and the fixed
  section order. Changed: the section list was extended with Responsive
  Behavior and Agent Prompt Guide, and two routes into the file were added:
  codifying a system that exists, and proposing a new one. Its npm package
  @google/design.md is called as a tool, never vendored. Apache-2.0 requires
  that modifications be marked, which this entry does.

- VoltAgent/awesome-design-md, MIT.
  https://github.com/VoltAgent/awesome-design-md
  Taken: the longer section list, specifically Responsive Behavior and Agent
  Prompt Guide, and the practice of describing a direction as mood plus
  accent plus type in one line. Changed: merged onto Google's token schema
  rather than used as prose alone. None of its 84 collected DESIGN.md files
  is reproduced, and each remains its own brand's property.

- Nutlope/hallmark, MIT. https://github.com/Nutlope/hallmark
  Taken: the three-tier gate model in references/gates.md — a floor that is
  never waivable, a reflex tier waivable in a comment with a specific reason,
  and a finish tier that is answered rather than blocking. Also the practice
  of building the most important screen alone and reviewing it with fresh
  context before the rest. Several individual rules were adapted: no invented
  metrics, no fake send, tinting away from pure black and white, overflow-x
  clip on both html and body, minmax(0, 1fr) on image tracks, dvh over vh,
  and the ban on the tracked-capitals label. Changed: rewritten in our own
  words and cut from 85 numbered gates to the ones that name a real harm. Its
  50 themes, its scripts, its stamp format and its waiver syntax are not
  reproduced.

- nexu-io/open-design, Apache-2.0. https://github.com/nexu-io/open-design
  Taken: the pipeline shape, brief to direction to design system to artifact,
  and the fork between codifying an existing brand and picking a new
  direction. Also the rule that a project's own design tokens outrank any
  universal craft rule. Changed: reduced to stages 1, 3 and 6 of SKILL.md.
  Where its craft rules require letter-spacing on capitals, this skill
  disagrees and says so in references/gates.md rather than hiding the
  conflict. None of its application, its 151 design-system packages or its
  plugin catalogue is reused.

- greensock/gsap-skills, MIT. https://github.com/greensock/gsap-skills
  Taken: only the rules that hold for plain CSS — animate transform and
  opacity rather than width, height, top or left; honour
  prefers-reduced-motion by setting the duration to zero; and never let a
  hidden element keep intercepting clicks. Changed: folded into
  references/gates.md and references/craft.md without any GSAP dependency.
  GSAP became free for all uses, every plugin included, after Webflow's
  acquisition; confirmed at gsap.com/pricing.

Read and deliberately not adapted:

- DavidHDev/react-bits and DavidHDev/canvas-ui, both MIT + Commons Clause.
  The Commons Clause permits use inside an application, website or product
  but forbids selling, sublicensing or redistributing the components
  themselves, alone, bundled or ported. Nothing from either is reproduced
  here. GitHub reports both as NOASSERTION, so ui-find names them as the
  worked example of why a licence must be read in the repository's own
  LICENSE file rather than taken from the sidebar label.

- oso95/scroll-world, MIT, and enesozturk/dimensional-drawing, MIT. Read in
  full. Scroll-world is a paid video-generation pipeline whose costs are
  stated at roughly 27 US dollars for a six-scene page, and its one
  transferable rule — scroll-linked motion must be a pure function of scroll
  position so that it plays correctly in reverse — is noted in
  references/craft.md. Dimensional-drawing produces specification sheets as
  inline SVG and is a tool to reach for, not a rule set to absorb.

Referred to rather than reused:

Public standards, which are not anybody's skill: WCAG 2.2 AA contrast ratios,
WCAG 2.5.8 target size, and the 44 by 44 point target from Apple's Human
Interface Guidelines.

Data fetched at run time, not redistributed here: the shadcn registry
directory at https://ui.shadcn.com/r/registries.json and each registry's own
catalogue. sources.json records what was read about each source, including its
licence; it contains no component code.
